{
  "id": "Compound::DB00820 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Tadalafil, Avanafil, and Vardenafil, all phosphodiesterase inhibitors, share a common mechanism of action involving binding to Cyp3a4, a key enzyme in drug metabolism. The evidence suggests that Tadalafil causes Penile Haemorrhage, which is then mediated by Avanafil or Vardenafil, both of which bind to Cyp3a4. This indicates a potential pathway where Tadalafil indirectly influences Cyp3a4 through the activation of Avanafil or Vardenafil, which in turn modulate the enzyme's activity. The shared lowest common ancestors—Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compounds and Cgmp Phosphodiesterase Inhibitors—highlight a common pharmacological class, reinforcing the link between these drugs and Cyp3a4. The integration of these pathways suggests that Tadalafil may affect Cyp3a4 via the activation of downstream compounds, potentially altering drug metabolism and contributing to its pharmacological effects.",
